290
01:72:30,260 --> 01:72:33,140
for yourself, I offer you a price good enough to get you

291
01:72:33,140 --> 01:72:33,780
to go and trade with me.

292
01:72:33,780 --> 01:72:36,140
Right? And it's exactly the same.

293
01:72:36,140 --> 01:72:39,700
If I say, you know, if I say 50-50, you say 90%,

294
01:72:40,020 --> 01:72:42,120
then you should be happy to give me two to one odds in order

295
01:72:42,120 --> 01:72:43,460
to induce me to bet with you.

296
01:72:44,160 --> 01:72:46,920
And if you don't want to give odds like that, that is a sign

297
01:72:46,920 --> 01:72:48,000
that you don't have the confidence

298
01:72:48,000 --> 01:72:48,960
that we are planning to have.

299
01:72:49,880 --> 01:72:53,360
So, what I say is voter rationality is, first of all,

300
01:72:53,360 --> 01:72:55,480
plausible in theory because of this.

301
01:72:56,540 --> 01:72:59,340
You know, democracy is exactly the kind of institutional setting

302
01:72:59,340 --> 01:73:01,120
where people can be completely crazy

303
01:73:01,120 --> 01:73:03,360
without any noticeable effect on themselves.

304
01:73:03,360 --> 01:73:04,880
And first of all, and second of all,

305
01:73:04,880 --> 01:73:07,160
it's also easy to detect empirically on a large scale.

306
01:73:07,840 --> 01:73:10,200
In fact, our stereotypes that economists have

307
01:73:10,200 --> 01:73:13,260
about public opinion and public beliefs about economics,

308
01:73:13,600 --> 01:73:15,640
almost all are confirmed by systematic data.

309
01:73:17,180 --> 01:73:19,960
And if you take public choice class,

310
01:73:19,960 --> 01:73:22,280
you'll go over a bunch of other stereotypes

311
01:73:22,280 --> 01:73:24,300
about how people are clueless and how right those are.

312
01:73:25,040 --> 01:73:29,240
One fun one is there is a stereotype that there's a lot

313
01:73:29,240 --> 01:73:31,800
of that all you need to do

314
01:73:31,800 --> 01:73:33,700
to make a war popular is just declare war

315
01:73:33,700 --> 01:73:35,540
and then people rally around and wave flags.

316
01:73:36,100 --> 01:73:38,200
And this is very strongly borne out by the data.

317
01:73:38,920 --> 01:73:40,400
It's called the rally around the flag effect.

318
01:73:40,400 --> 01:73:41,920
If you just take a look at public opinion,

319
01:73:42,400 --> 01:73:45,680
even if the last poll before war is declared makes the war

320
01:73:45,680 --> 01:73:48,840
unpopular, just declaring it causes a big spike

321
01:73:48,840 --> 01:73:50,960
in popularity and then makes it popular.

322
01:73:52,560 --> 01:73:55,320
So, you know, like emotionally this makes perfect sense, right?

323
01:73:55,800 --> 01:73:57,460
Because you hear your country's at war

324
01:73:57,460 --> 01:73:59,720
and what is a normal human being's emotional reaction

325
01:73:59,720 --> 01:74:00,040
to that?

326
01:74:01,820 --> 01:74:03,800
USA, USA, right?

327
01:74:03,800 --> 01:74:05,140
Well, Americans' reaction.

328
01:74:05,560 --> 01:74:08,040
Like say, whatever country we're in, Guatemala.

329
01:74:08,040 --> 01:74:12,540
All right, so these are normal human reactions.

330
01:74:12,540 --> 01:74:15,980
You hear your country's at war and you want to support them

331
01:74:15,980 --> 01:74:18,360
and part of supporting them is saying it's a great idea

332
01:74:18,360 --> 01:74:19,400
and that we're going to win.

333
01:74:20,400 --> 01:74:21,820
Right? You've probably heard about the beginning

334
01:74:21,820 --> 01:74:22,520
of World War I.

335
01:74:22,520 --> 01:74:24,380
There are parades in all the big countries.

336
01:74:24,380 --> 01:74:27,040
Happy parades of imminent victory.

337
01:74:28,040 --> 01:74:28,760
They're all wrong.

338
01:74:29,320 --> 01:74:31,520
Right? Now, you might say, well, maybe some people thought

339
01:74:31,520 --> 01:74:32,860
that they were really likely to win.

340
01:74:33,080 --> 01:74:34,840
Yeah, but everyone seems to think they were likely to win.

341
01:74:35,560 --> 01:74:36,800
And that doesn't make sense.

342
01:74:37,300 --> 01:74:38,660
You know, if you're in France and you say, well,

343
01:74:39,040 --> 01:74:39,820
why are we so happy?

344
01:74:39,820 --> 01:74:41,200
The Germans are having a really big parade.

345
01:74:41,200 --> 01:74:42,300
Maybe they didn't beat us last time

346
01:74:42,300 --> 01:74:43,200
and they're going to beat us again.

347
01:74:43,900 --> 01:74:46,280
Right? That is not, that is the kind of thing

348
01:74:46,280 --> 01:74:48,480
that is going to make enemies and it's also the kind

349
01:74:48,480 --> 01:74:51,480
of thing that makes you feel like a traitor, even to think.

350
01:74:51,480 --> 01:74:54,540
Right? You know, just the thought of our country can lose.

351
01:74:55,360 --> 01:74:57,460
Those people, to think things like this normally.

352
01:74:58,060 --> 01:75:00,480
And things that hurt you to believe are things

353
01:75:00,480 --> 01:75:01,960
that people tend not to believe.

354
01:75:03,860 --> 01:75:07,660
Right? Now, Whitman is always demanding, if you read him,

355
01:75:08,220 --> 01:75:10,100
for, he's demanding those critics say,

356
01:75:10,100 --> 01:75:13,360
what is the key asymmetry between markets and politics?

357
01:75:13,640 --> 01:75:14,800
How are they really different?

358
01:75:14,800 --> 01:75:18,160
Isn't it all just a competitive interaction

359
01:75:18,160 --> 01:75:21,140
where people are trying to please customers or voters

360
01:75:21,140 --> 01:75:24,020
and why don't, why wouldn't both systems work equally well,

361
01:75:24,020 --> 01:75:25,020
at least within their sphere?

362
01:75:25,740 --> 01:75:29,940
And my answer is the key asymmetry is incentives

363
01:75:29,940 --> 01:75:30,560
for rationality.

364
01:75:31,740 --> 01:75:34,340
So, in markets, there are plenty of people

365
01:75:34,340 --> 01:75:35,940
who I will say are still irrational.

366
01:75:36,260 --> 01:75:39,540
But at least if you're rational, you get something out of it.

367
01:75:39,580 --> 01:75:41,180
You can avoid harm to yourself.

368
01:75:41,580 --> 01:75:42,600
You can get a better deal.

369
01:75:43,480 --> 01:75:45,260
Right? Whereas in politics,

370
01:75:45,600 --> 01:75:48,820
if you are the lone rational person, what happens to you?

371
01:75:48,820 --> 01:75:51,640
Same thing that happens to everybody else.

372
01:75:51,640 --> 01:75:54,380
Right? So, in markets, even when people are ignorant,

373
01:75:54,380 --> 01:75:56,100
they generally try to apply common sense,

374
01:75:56,100 --> 01:75:58,340
which is why internet scams generally don't work.

375
01:75:59,480 --> 01:76:03,320
Right? So, you know, you may not know a lot of details

376
01:76:03,320 --> 01:76:07,560
about Nigeria or why there's so many spam emails from them.

377
01:76:08,060 --> 01:76:09,540
But you do have common sense.

378
01:76:09,540 --> 01:76:11,280
And common sense tells you to delete those people.

379
01:76:12,200 --> 01:76:14,100
Right? And what are some common sense sayings

380
01:76:14,100 --> 01:76:16,740
that would advise you to go and just delete them?

381
01:76:16,740 --> 01:76:22,780
How about if it sounds too good to be true, it probably is.

382
01:76:23,320 --> 01:76:24,320
That's just common sense.

383
01:76:24,800 --> 01:76:26,720
Doesn't require much information to apply it.

384
01:76:26,720 --> 01:76:27,840
It's just like, who are you?

385
01:76:28,140 --> 01:76:28,720
I don't know you.

386
01:76:29,260 --> 01:76:30,720
You're offering me something that sounds really great.

387
01:76:31,100 --> 01:76:31,700
I don't believe it.

388
01:76:31,920 --> 01:76:33,100
Delete. Right?

389
01:76:33,100 --> 01:76:34,060
People do this all the time.

390
01:76:34,680 --> 01:76:38,260
But politicians do the same kind of thing, and it works.

391
01:76:38,720 --> 01:76:41,520
Politician comes along and says, this is going to be fantastic.

392
01:76:41,520 --> 01:76:42,960
I'm going to give you this great deal.

393
01:76:43,260 --> 01:76:46,000
Like, we're going to, like, everyone will be insured.

394
01:76:46,000 --> 01:76:47,320
The costs will go down.

395
01:76:47,320 --> 01:76:48,600
It's going to be wonderful.

396
01:76:49,040 --> 01:76:50,320
Right? And people believe them.

397
01:76:50,740 --> 01:76:53,940
Or someone says, look, we're going to go and invade Iraq.

398
01:76:54,300 --> 01:76:55,980
We're going to put a stall on democracy.

399
01:76:56,200 --> 01:76:57,700
And we're going to bring peace to the Middle East.

400
01:76:57,780 --> 01:76:59,160
And it's going to happen in our time.

401
01:76:59,160 --> 01:76:59,900
And we're going to do it.

402
01:76:59,900 --> 01:77:00,840
And you get to be part of it.

403
01:77:01,240 --> 01:77:03,000
That whole story, that sounds like a great story.

404
01:77:03,000 --> 01:77:03,840
It's going to be cheap.

405
01:77:03,840 --> 01:77:05,000
Cheap, fast.

406
01:77:05,560 --> 01:77:06,280
Right? Or let's see.

407
01:77:06,640 --> 01:77:08,720
Last night, I was seeing a clip

408
01:77:08,820 --> 01:77:11,900
from John F. Kennedy's Cuban Missile Crisis speech.

409
01:77:11,900 --> 01:77:16,060
And one line, I think it's like, this is almost word for word.

410
01:77:16,060 --> 01:77:17,480
He said, you know, the greatest risk

411
01:77:17,480 --> 01:77:18,520
of all would be to do nothing.

412
01:77:19,900 --> 01:77:20,160
That's it.

413
01:77:20,660 --> 01:77:21,740
How do you know?

414
01:77:21,740 --> 01:77:23,300
I mean, this never happened before.

415
01:77:23,300 --> 01:77:26,060
It seems like there's a really, there's one scenario

416
01:77:26,060 --> 01:77:27,940
where this causes a nuclear war,

417
01:77:27,940 --> 01:77:29,560
which we could have avoided just by appeasement.

418
01:77:30,400 --> 01:77:32,740
Maybe it'll be even worse if we don't fight now.

419
01:77:32,740 --> 01:77:34,120
But then again, maybe not.

420
01:77:34,660 --> 01:77:35,460
How do you really know?

421
01:77:36,120 --> 01:77:38,240
What, like, where's the, well, like, can we have a bet on this?

422
01:77:38,240 --> 01:77:40,700
And, you know, we, but, you know, none of that is there.

423
01:77:40,700 --> 01:77:41,900
And Stan, he just says that.

424
01:77:42,260 --> 01:77:44,660
And again, when, and of course, he says stuff like this

425
01:77:44,660 --> 01:77:45,700
because he knows, you know,

426
01:77:45,700 --> 01:77:47,740
as a very successful politician, this appeals to people.

427
01:77:48,280 --> 01:77:51,460
They want, you know, a forceful leader who tells them,

428
01:77:51,460 --> 01:77:52,820
like, we are going to act.

429
01:77:53,120 --> 01:77:54,600
We are going to solve the problem.

430
01:77:54,960 --> 01:77:58,600
And those naysayers who just want to leave well enough alone

431
01:77:59,000 --> 01:78:02,240
are going to make the whole, can only make the problem worse.

432
01:78:02,800 --> 01:78:06,180
Not just to say might or seems likely to be, you know,

433
01:78:06,180 --> 01:78:08,000
the kind of thing that would be the honest thing to say.

434
01:78:08,700 --> 01:78:09,820
Right? Say, well, you know, and again,

435
01:78:09,820 --> 01:78:12,980
just imagine a president saying, look, I've crunched the numbers.

436
01:78:13,400 --> 01:78:16,200
There's a 40% chance my action improves things.

437
01:78:16,620 --> 01:78:18,300
40% chance it leaves them the same.

438
01:78:18,300 --> 01:78:20,100
20% chance that it makes it worse.

439
01:78:20,520 --> 01:78:21,680
It's a favorable bet.

440
01:78:22,220 --> 01:78:22,860
So let's do it.

441
01:78:23,780 --> 01:78:25,120
That is not a war president.

442
01:78:25,600 --> 01:78:27,920
That is not someone who is rallying a country behind him

443
01:78:27,920 --> 01:78:28,520
for anything.

444
01:78:29,360 --> 01:78:31,580
Instead, people like a leader just says, look,

445
01:78:32,060 --> 01:78:34,020
victory is ours if we believe it.

446
01:78:36,120 --> 01:78:39,800
Dot. Like, okay, like, is that generally true?

447
01:78:39,800 --> 01:78:41,020
Is that only our country?

448
01:78:41,020 --> 01:78:42,960
Does every country get this amazing,

449
01:78:43,340 --> 01:78:46,660
this amazing Tinkerbell principle that if we just believe

450
01:78:46,660 --> 01:78:48,080
in fairies that it happens?

451
01:78:48,080 --> 01:78:49,040
No. Wrong.

452
01:78:49,580 --> 01:78:53,860
But, like, why would politicians say this ridiculous nonsense?

453
01:78:53,860 --> 01:78:54,880
And not just one person.

454
01:78:54,880 --> 01:78:55,520
They all do it.

455
01:78:56,040 --> 01:78:57,980
Why do they all say this ridiculous nonsense?

456
01:78:59,380 --> 01:79:01,400
Right? You know, like, just a bunch of literal,

457
01:79:01,400 --> 01:79:03,520
one literal falsehood after another.

458
01:79:04,500 --> 01:79:06,620
Right? And people believe this stuff.

459
01:79:06,660 --> 01:79:07,340
People like it.

460
01:79:07,340 --> 01:79:10,200
You know, maybe part of it is people are just, well,

461
01:79:10,200 --> 01:79:11,260
like, just like poetry.

462
01:79:11,900 --> 01:79:13,840
But, again, I've talked to enough people

463
01:79:13,840 --> 01:79:16,900
who tell me the poems with very, with great seriousness

464
01:79:16,900 --> 01:79:18,280
as if they've got it all figured out.

465
01:79:19,040 --> 01:79:20,820
I think that they actually believe the poems.

466
01:79:21,500 --> 01:79:24,180
Like, you know, it's, someone believes a poem is

467
01:79:24,180 --> 01:79:24,920
literally true.

468
01:79:24,920 --> 01:79:26,960
That's what it looks like to me.

469
01:79:26,960 --> 01:79:29,940
Like, every time someone says, this can only result in this.

470
01:79:30,740 --> 01:79:32,660
So you'll bet me on, like, a million to one odds.

471
01:79:32,660 --> 01:79:34,600
Or, no, even odds.

472
01:79:34,600 --> 01:79:35,280
Or no bet at all.

473
01:79:35,280 --> 01:79:39,280
Wow. One plus one can only equal two.

474
01:79:39,280 --> 01:79:40,820
Now, that I'll bet a lot of money on.

475
01:79:41,440 --> 01:79:42,920
But they don't want to bet a lot of money

476
01:79:42,920 --> 01:79:45,260
on all the things they claim can only be a certain way.

477
01:79:46,260 --> 01:79:46,440
All right.

478
01:79:46,940 --> 01:79:50,700
Now, last thing that we get out of my rationality story is

479
01:79:50,700 --> 01:79:52,760
it explains why a successful politician doesn't try

480
01:79:52,760 --> 01:79:53,640
to fix the voters.

481
01:79:54,460 --> 01:79:56,160
Successful politician isn't out to educate.

482
01:79:56,680 --> 01:79:58,360
Successful politician is out to pander.

483
01:79:58,360 --> 01:80:05,360
To tell people what they want to hear.


1
01:80:00,000 --> 01:80:00,560
want to hear.

2
01:80:01,420 --> 01:80:03,980
Right? And let's see, anyone here ever read Dale Carnegie's

3
01:80:03,980 --> 01:80:05,360
How to Win Friends and Influence People?

4
01:80:06,180 --> 01:80:08,080
You really don't have to read the book in order to get one

5
01:80:08,080 --> 01:80:09,660
of the main punchlines almost immediately.

6
01:80:10,420 --> 01:80:13,920
So, if you could A, tell people what they want to hear,

7
01:80:14,840 --> 01:80:16,640
or B, tell them what they don't want to hear,

8
01:80:16,960 --> 01:80:18,760
which is the better approach for making them like you?

9
01:80:19,960 --> 01:80:21,680
Tell them what they do want to hear.

10
01:80:22,100 --> 01:80:23,800
Well, what if what they want to hear is false?

11
01:80:24,760 --> 01:80:25,800
Yeah, then lie.

12
01:80:26,280 --> 01:80:28,540
Right? Or, of course, the easy thing is just don't investigate

13
01:80:28,540 --> 01:80:30,900
very closely, and then it's just speaking without,

14
01:80:31,100 --> 01:80:33,000
you know, it's like, well, it could be true, who knows?

15
01:80:34,420 --> 01:80:34,760
All right.

16
01:80:35,220 --> 01:80:37,620
So, anyway, voters who, voters like candidates

17
01:80:37,620 --> 01:80:39,980
who share the confusions, they don't want pedants

18
01:80:39,980 --> 01:80:40,920
who are going to lecture them.

19
01:80:41,420 --> 01:80:43,800
Can you imagine any major politician coming up

20
01:80:43,800 --> 01:80:45,700
and giving a speech before the American public,

21
01:80:46,060 --> 01:80:47,620
especially in a debate or something like that,

22
01:80:47,620 --> 01:80:49,520
and saying, look, I understand

23
01:80:49,520 --> 01:80:51,300
that most Americans are extremely concerned

24
01:80:51,300 --> 01:80:52,880
about the effects of international trade.

25
01:80:53,160 --> 01:80:54,360
They're worried about the hauling

26
01:80:54,360 --> 01:80:55,400
out of American industry.

27
01:80:55,780 --> 01:80:56,940
They're afraid for their future.

28
01:80:56,940 --> 01:80:59,560
I understand all this, but I'm here

29
01:80:59,560 --> 01:81:00,700
to tell you these fears are wrong.

30
01:81:02,320 --> 01:81:03,520
Like, you know, imagine, though,

31
01:81:03,520 --> 01:81:05,380
like what people would say to something like that,

32
01:81:05,840 --> 01:81:06,920
and then say, and here's why.

33
01:81:07,000 --> 01:81:09,120
Well, let's just go over to the Econ textbook here,

34
01:81:09,380 --> 01:81:11,380
page 233, comparative advantage.

35
01:81:11,380 --> 01:81:13,300
Let's go through this stuff, and we're, all right,

36
01:81:13,540 --> 01:81:15,000
and you have lost everybody.

37
01:81:16,040 --> 01:81:18,600
Even if you give the best version of comparative advantage

38
01:81:18,600 --> 01:81:21,240
that I've ever heard, which is the Iowa car crop,

39
01:81:22,120 --> 01:81:24,700
your most likely reaction is your opponent saying, look,

40
01:81:24,700 --> 01:81:27,680
so you think you can turn corn into cars, huh?

41
01:81:28,100 --> 01:81:30,120
Well, that sounds like an amazing magic trick,

42
01:81:30,120 --> 01:81:32,040
but most of us live in reality here

43
01:81:33,020 --> 01:81:34,680
where you can't turn corn into cars.

44
01:81:35,220 --> 01:81:38,620
You say, look, it's, you've lost the debate,

45
01:81:38,620 --> 01:81:41,760
you've lost the election, and you're out of luck, right?

46
01:81:41,760 --> 01:81:43,820
So, and instead, what you really want

47
01:81:43,820 --> 01:81:46,640
to say in an election is American people believe these

48
01:81:46,640 --> 01:81:48,780
things, and I believe them, too, with all my heart,

49
01:81:48,780 --> 01:81:50,240
and I will take decisive action.

50
01:81:51,820 --> 01:81:54,220
One other example that you almost never hear a politician

51
01:81:54,220 --> 01:81:57,140
saying, look, this is a horrible, terrible problem.

52
01:81:57,720 --> 01:81:59,620
Unfortunately, I've studied this issue very closely,

53
01:81:59,620 --> 01:82:01,200
and there's not a damn thing government can do about it,

54
01:82:01,800 --> 01:82:02,920
so we just have to live with the problem.

55
01:82:03,740 --> 01:82:07,460
I'll keep my ears, I'll keep my ears perked in case a new idea

56
01:82:07,460 --> 01:82:11,300
comes along, but any, if my rival says he can solve this

57
01:82:11,300 --> 01:82:12,160
for you, he's lying.

58
01:82:13,440 --> 01:82:16,960
No one ever says that, right?

59
01:82:16,960 --> 01:82:19,340
And it's got to be true sometimes, right?

60
01:82:19,340 --> 01:82:20,900
It can't always be wrong.

61
01:82:20,900 --> 01:82:22,900
It can't be that every problem people are talking

62
01:82:22,900 --> 01:82:26,780
about has a viable solution, but people don't like hearing

63
01:82:26,780 --> 01:82:29,420
that a problem doesn't have a solution, and so they vote

